# Line-ending policy for files whose bytes are semantically load-bearing.
#
# The repository is developed on Windows and validated on Linux, and git's
# `core.autocrlf` default would rewrite these on checkout.  For the hook scripts
# that is a correctness problem, not a cosmetic one: a POSIX shell script whose
# shebang line ends in CRLF does not run, so a hook that works on the development
# machine would fail on a Linux clone -- which is exactly the environment the
# acceptance track uses.

.githooks/* text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# The published acceptance bundle is hashed, and the hashes are the point: the
# artifact manifest lists a sha256 per document, so a checkout that rewrote line
# endings would produce a bundle whose own manifest disagrees with it.  A clean
# checkout failed on exactly that before this rule existed -- the manifest was
# written from LF bytes on the development machine and then verified against CRLF
# bytes in the clone.  These documents are written with LF and checked out with LF,
# so their digests are the same in every checkout.
acceptance/v0.4.2/*.md text eol=lf
acceptance/v0.4.2/*.json text eol=lf
